+# BLOCKED: a declarative `NOT NULL` / `UNIQUE` on `users.email` (and the data cleanup behind it)
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` now **enforces** "email is required and unique,
+ignoring case" on every write path, so `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` and
+`test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` both pass and no customer row is modified or
+deleted. This file documents the part that is still blocked.
+
+## What is blocked
+
+A *declarative* constraint — `email TEXT NOT NULL` plus
+`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... (email COLLATE NOCASE)` — cannot exist while the
+production data still violates it. SQLite validates a `UNIQUE` index against
+existing rows at creation time, and `NOT NULL` can only be introduced by
+rebuilding the table and re-inserting every row. Both require the offending rows
+to be fixed first, and fixing them is a data-ownership decision (Support owns
+`users`), not something a schema migration may do.
+
+The snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`, 40 rows) violates the target rule:
+
+| id | email                   | name          | problem                              |
+|----|-------------------------|---------------|--------------------------------------|
+| 17 | `NULL`                  | Goran Okafor  | violates "required"                  |
+| 1  | `ana.silva@example.com` | Ana Silva     | duplicate (see below)                |
+| 4  | `Ana.Silva@example.com` | Dev Silva     | duplicate of id 1 ignoring case      |
+| 23 | `ana.silva@example.com` | Ana Silva     | exact duplicate of id 1              |
+
+## What the migration does instead
+
+Because a table rebuild / unique index is impossible without touching those rows,
+`002_unique_email.sql` installs `BEFORE INSERT` / `BEFORE UPDATE` triggers that
+reject a NULL email, or an email equal (ignoring case) to an existing row's.
+This is real, database-level enforcement for all future writes, and it reads but
+never modifies existing rows. Verified:
+
+- prod snapshot rows are byte-for-byte identical before and after applying 002;
+- `INSERT`/`UPDATE` with `NULL`, and with a case-only duplicate, raise
+  `sqlite3.IntegrityError`;
+- unrelated `UPDATE`s (e.g. changing `name`) still work.
+
+## What Support must do to unblock the declarative constraint
+
+1. Resolve the `ana.silva@example.com` collision (ids 1, 4, 23).
+2. Give id 17 a real, unique email (or remove the account, if intended).
+
+Reference queries (run by hand — **not** part of the migration):
+
+```sql
+SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE email IS NULL;
+
+SELECT lower(email) AS e, COUNT(*) AS n, group_concat(id) AS ids
+FROM users WHERE email IS NOT NULL
+GROUP BY lower(email) HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;
+```
+
+Once no row violates the rule, the data can be given a real
+`NOT NULL` + case-insensitive `UNIQUE` constraint (dropping the triggers at that
+point). Until then, the triggers keep new data compliant.
